# Naming and readability

## Descriptive names over abbreviations

Optimize for the next reader, not fewer keystrokes. Avoid single-letter names
outside tight loop counters, and avoid ambiguous abbreviations.

```typescript
// BAD
const d = calcDiff(a, b);

// GOOD
const businessHoursRemaining = calculateBusinessHoursRemaining(start, deadline);
```

## Functions do one thing

If a function name needs "and" to describe it, split it. A function that
validates *and* saves *and* notifies should be three functions composed by a
caller, so each piece is independently testable and readable.

## Comments explain why, not what

Do not narrate code in comments — the code already says what it does. Only
comment on non-obvious intent, trade-offs, or constraints the code can't
express on its own.

```typescript
// BAD
// increment the counter
counter++;

// GOOD
// osTicket's legacy API returns 1-indexed page numbers; keep parity with it here.
page++;
```

## Delete dead code instead of commenting it out

Commented-out code rots and confuses future readers about whether it's safe
to remove. Delete it — version control already remembers it.
